sections.s2_technical:
The core idea: add a column like `deleted BOOLEAN` or `deleted_at TIMESTAMP`, and make the application treat flagged rows as gone.

`@SQLDelete` intercepts the DELETE Hibernate would issue and replaces it with your SQL, typically an UPDATE that sets the flag. So when you call `repository.delete(entity)` or `em.remove(entity)`, Hibernate runs `UPDATE accounts SET deleted = true WHERE id = ?` instead of a real DELETE.

```java
@Entity
@SQLDelete(sql = "UPDATE accounts SET deleted = true WHERE id = ?")
@SQLRestriction("deleted = false")   // Hibernate 6+ (replaces @Where)
public class Account { ... }
```

`@SQLRestriction` (or the older `@Where(clause = "deleted = false")`) appends a static predicate to *every* select for this entity, so normal `findById`, JPQL, and association loads automatically exclude deleted rows. The predicate is fixed at mapping time — you cannot turn it off per query.

`@Filter` is the flexible alternative. You define a `@FilterDef` with parameters and a `@Filter` condition, then enable it per session with a value. This lets you decide at runtime whether to include deleted rows — for example, an admin "show archived" view enables the filter with `includeDeleted = true`.

```java
@FilterDef(name = "activeFilter", parameters = @ParamDef(name = "isDeleted", type = Boolean.class))
@Filter(name = "activeFilter", condition = "deleted = :isDeleted")
// enable per session:
session.enableFilter("activeFilter").setParameter("isDeleted", false);
```

`@SoftDelete` (Hibernate 6.4+) is the modern one-annotation approach. Put `@SoftDelete` on the entity and Hibernate manages the deleted column, rewrites deletes to updates, and filters selects automatically — no manual `@SQLDelete` + `@SQLRestriction` pairing. You can choose the column, the strategy (active-flag or deleted-flag), and the converter.

```java
@Entity
@SoftDelete(columnName = "deleted", strategy = SoftDeleteType.DELETED)
public class Account { ... }
```

Trade-offs and nuances (senior level): unique constraints become tricky because soft-deleted rows still occupy the table, so a unique email constraint blocks re-creating a "deleted" account unless you include the deleted flag in the constraint or use partial indexes. Static `@SQLRestriction` can hurt when you legitimately need deleted rows (audits, restores) — `@Filter` is better there. Soft delete bloats tables and indexes over time, so you often pair it with periodic archival/purge. Cascade behaviour needs care: deleting a parent should soft-delete children consistently. And native queries bypass `@SQLRestriction`, so they can accidentally return deleted rows.

